  "notes": "Two houses and a shared pool along the edge of a slope falling to the west. From the west views (long bars of dark site stone, flush concrete roof, horizontal strip and square windows, the whole volume standing on a concrete floor slab that runs out past the stone with the supports recessed into shadow over the hillside), the corner and dusk views (a big roof slab thrown out over a glazed living room at the end of each house, a stone-parapet balcony cantilevered on the downhill corner, steps down to the ground), the gallery views (roof overhanging the east side over a timber deck, deep concrete beams running out east over the bedroom patios, carrying wires for climbers), the roof view (gravel roof inside a concrete upstand, U-shaped planter beams) and the pool view (the pool raised, stone-clad on a concrete base). The slope is a bank rising east to a level terrace; the living slab rises toward the east as the article describes. Which end is the living room, the second house's length (~14 m against ~24 m) and the pool sitting on the east terrace between them are read from the distant views and guessed; plans were not shown. Scrub is shown as trees and bushes; the pirca (dry-stone wall) below the houses is a straight run. Refinement pass (24 Sep 2026): the living roofs are now thin tilted slabs (shed with thick, 0.45 m, rising 0.5 m to the east) instead of solid wedges, with the gap over the stone walls closed by a concrete fillet. The hillside was too gentle to show the suspension: the east terrace is now 3 m up, the bank falls 3 m to the west over 17 m, and the houses (floor slab, stone, roofs, pool) were raised with it, so the concrete floor slab hovers about 1.5 m over the falling ground at the west edge with its supports set back in shadow. The ground is grass (the photos show a green meadow, not bare earth) with the bank in dry grass and pale granite boulders as in the corner view; the scrub trees are umbrella-crowned (espinillo, chañar), with three more on the lower ground and bushes on the bank. The stone is rubble (grey-brown field stone) and reads darker than the concrete slabs. Still guessed: the real fall of the ground (drawn as one even bank), the boulders' and trees' positions, the timber-slat soffit of the gallery (not drawn; it cannot be seen from above), and the steps down from the living-room corner."
